{
  "dom_id": "step-q274614",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x-\u03d5)$ with $0<\u03d5<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $\u03d5?$\n\n$\u03d5=$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The difference formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x-\u03d5) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)+R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)=R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x-\u03d5),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)=(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)+(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5}{R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\u03d5 & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \u03d5 & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ \u03d5 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
